From caf-talk Caf May 17 16:38:19 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: U15289@UICVM.UIC.EDU (Mitch Pravatiner)
Subject: If it ain't broke...
Message-ID: <199305172038.AA14079@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 17 May 1993 20:04:31 GMT

Discussions in this list growing out of the McElwaine affair have included
speculations on such possible policy responses as sysadmins enforcing news-
group charters, and penalizing off-topic posts on those grounds; limiting the
number of posts per day from a single user; limiting the length of individual
posts; etc.  These are overreactions to an extreme case, and should not be
adopted.

A posting may not be precisely congruent with the specific theme of a group,
but still have a rational nexus thereto.  This makes the question of whether
the posting is truly off-topic ambiguous, in some cases.  But apart from that,
to declare an off-topic posting actionable _per se_ creates an opportunity
try to circumvent the First Amendment, by offering a narrow technical pretext
for penalizing the expression of disvalued ideas.

Limiting the number or length of a user's postings could indeed eliminate a
potential source of inefficiency in the operation of news systems; but the
cure would be worse than the disease.  A prolific writer of long articles, who
posts them in good faith, should not be stifled in the sweet name of avoiding
the nuisances arguably caused by the antics of a McElwaine.

McElwaine's posting behavior may indeed have created technical difficulties for
some Usenet sites, and increased the costs of newsreading for some users.  It
may be plausible to argue that he acted recklessly, and that the sanctions
against him were justified.  For all that, generalization from this single,
eccentric case should not be the basis for general policy.  Simple common
sense may not always suffice as a building-block for rule making and enforce-
ment, but it can still go along way.  The letter and spirit of the Winona State
AUP--which takes both the actual effect, and the actual intent, of a user's
behavior into account in determining whether it constitutes a violation--seems
to me to be the ideal way to go.

