Whether the hair of African Americans is a sound topic to build a course around
depends a lot on the frame of reference.  I can see how this particular phenom-
enon could lend itself to being the stuff that curricular _Guano del Toro_ :-)
is made of; and yet there is a sense in which it could be the takeoff point for
genuine intellectual inquiry.

Negroid hair, as one physical racial characteristic among many, is one of the
building blocks of the black race as a distinctive phenotypical entity.  In
the real world, of course, race is as much a social construct as a biological
one, and the physical characteristics of persons defined socioculturally as
"black" are quite diverse.  (F. James Davis' excellent book _Who Is Black?_
(University Park:  Pennsylvania State University Press, 1991) discusses these
phenomena at length.)  Insofar as hair can be considered a cultural artifact,
one of many factors in the social construction of the black community--both
its own construction of itself, and the way that other ethnoracial communities
construe it--it is an eminently legitimate subject to build a course around.

