In article <1thgbl$ml4@menudo.uh.edu> st10t@Rosie.UH.EDU writes:
[...]
>A while back, a friend of mine got his account shut down because he had
>dropped the class the account was assigned to him for.

Note that because the account was provided as a tool for the class, the student
did not have a "right" to the account.  Had the account been granted simply
because he was a student, as is often done automatically in some schools when
students enroll in classes, he might have a "right" to the account.  In this
case, access of the account was dependant on enrollment in a particular class.
This is an important distinction.

>He was given two hours to get his stuff out.

He was lucky to be given that much time.  I'm sure some schools wouldn't even
provide that courtesy.  I'm fairly confident that George Mason wouldn't provide
it.

>Being stupid, he decided to print out the /etc/passwd file out on the local
>public access printers.

Stupid because he printed it out on pubilc access printers, or simply because
he printed it at all?

>Now it is important to point out that the passwd file is shadowed.

I don't think it's important at all.

>Nonetheless the printout was confiscated with the allegation that he would
>decrypt passwords... 

Here at GMU, when a student receives an account, one of the rules that s/he
must agree to is to not access any files outside of his/her account without
*explicit* permission to do so.  This means that if a student here printed
out a copy of etc/passwd, regardless of whether it was shadowed or not, s/he
would be in violation of the stated policies, and may be subject to punishment.

Of course, such a policy it difficult and burdensome to police, so they're
pretty lax about it.  Additionally, so long as a student doesn't tamper with
anything, or use the information illegally or in additional violation of the
rules, there's little reason to care.  Enforcement of a policy in such a case
becomes more of a tool than a deterent, must like local sodomy laws -- such
laws are used mostly against rapists and the like, rather than on consenting
individuals.

Therefore, if the student agreed to a policy similar to what I've described
above, then there is certainly reason for the school to punish him for his
violation.

[...]
>The computing people said they would call her back and fifteen minutes
>later agreed with us, but then they stated that he shouldn't have been printing
>out the passwd file anyway and that this is "unauthorized use or misuse of 
>computer resources." 

Bingo.

>The finger port on the system is enabled and infact one can wildcard user
>accounts out via the finger port.  IE they seem not consider the information
>in the passwd file *secret* in anyway since it may be accessed via any
>computer system on the network.

Finger does not provide password information (encrypted or otherwise).  Finger
is an explicitly available service (you need only type the command at the shell
prompt to use it -- you don't have to go hunting for it like you do
/etc/passwd).  The information provided by finger is controllable by individual
users.  Information potentially in /etc/passwd (specifically, encryped
passwords) is not.

>We tried to point this out but the dean had gotten it into her head that
>since it was called a passwd file it must have passwds in it or at the least
>stuff we shouldn't be looking at.  IE didn't you KNOW since it was called
>PASSWD you shouldn't be accessing the file, was a typical question.

More importantly, because it wasn't in your account's directory tree, didn't
you know you shouldn't be using it without explicit permission?  Besides, it
DID have (encrypted) passwords in it -- there is no legitimate reason why
someone other than system management to have *else's* password, in any form.

>Up to this point nothing was very serious, after all he DID print out a
>password file when he SHOULD have been just taking his stuff out. 

Good point!  Another thing he did wrong.  Also, presumably, if access to an
account is suspened, then so is related access to other computing resources,
including printers.

>(He stated the reason he did it was that it was a convenient way of finding
>out how many users (some 200 most of which were grad. students and professors)
>have access to the only general use Unix machine in our school of 35000. And
>he was going to use the GECOS information to write a letter on the computing
>centers paranoid use of resources (of which all 35000 students pay student 
>services fees) This is a digression but I didn't want you all to hang him 
>in your own minds..

Oh puh-leeze.  If he wanted the number of users on the system, there are any
number of ways to get *just that*.  Why not just ask the computing center?
This is a cheesy excuse, and I think rightfully dismissed by the authorities.

>Needless to say he got a letter today in the mail stating that he was
>suspended for a YEAR and was prohibited from walking onto school property and
>that he had 5 days to appeal.

This seems a bit undue, despite the rules violations, I have to agree.  I
wonder if there's more information here that we aren't getting...

>He is very shaken and quite honestly at a loss for what to do at this point,
>the school has actively prevented him from achieving his degree.

Well, from the above, all they're doing is slowing him down, not preventing.
Besides, what do you think punishments are *supposed* to do -- *assist* him
in getting his degree?  If someone does something wrong, they should expect
at least a punishment that will inconvenience them, if not worse.

>Additionally he is PERMANENTLY banned from using computing facilities of the
>schools (even when he comes back.)  As he is a computer science major, this
>will make it rather difficult for him to take some of his classes.

That's too bad.  Maybe he'll think about this in the future when he pokes
around where he isn't allowed.

-Steve Coile

