st10t@Rosie.UH.EDU writes:
> As you say yourself, the information can be accessed by other utilities, thus
> the information in the /etc/passwd file is not sensitive, therefore what is
> wrong with printing it?  

Perhaps you should argue the other way round:  If /etc/passwd
contained sensitive data, it would be quite negligent to store
this data in a publicly accessible, well-known place.  Adding a
clause to some usage guidelines is not a solution.  Similarly,
if a company placed their personnel index into their entrance
hall, unlocked, noone would punish visitors glancing at the data;
instead, the responsible manager would be asked why s/he didn't
protect it appropriately.  You should ask the UH staff similar
questions.  If they still insist that /etc/passwd is sensitive --
their problem. ;-)  They cannot argue that you should have known
about the `restrictions':  Hackers trying to access sensitive
files don't read guidelines first...  Again, if someone in charge
of data he or she thinks is sensitive does not protect it against
real hackers, something is wrong.

Another aspect: German law restricts prosecution to cases where
"electronically stored data is _particularly_protected_ against
unauthorized access" (StGB, section 202a).  Because /etc/passwd
is not in any way protected, accessing it (even if it contains
encrypted passwords!) is not considered punishable in Germany.
Is there similar U.S. law?
(Yes, I know that there is a difference between federal law and
university directives, but a university should be able to list
good reasons if their guidelines are inconsistent with other law.)

CU, Carsten

