st10t@Rosie.UH.EDU wrote:

: 	Dear world of usenet,

: 	The response from the network community has been tremendous,
: in fact several of you have requested addresses to which to write letters
: to and so in kind I will post several addresses and give a general assessement
: of what results may come out.

Wait a second here.

I don't know about anyone else, but I plan no involvement in this.
First of all, this is the net. With no intent to intimate that we
have been lied to or anything else, we don't really know all there
is to know about this.

What we do know is that a student was told to clear his PERSONAL
materials from a computer account, and he purposely dumped out the
passwd file. Yes, I KNOW that there is very little valuable info
to be gained --except... The names in the password file are there
specifically to identify participants in the computer system. Yes,
they can be 'fingered' and even read by those who wish to cat the
file while online. However, each of the students in there also has 
a right to a certain amount of privacy, and so I look at anyone
removing those names the same way I would anybody building a name
list --even electronic.

When I was in college, the school was forbidden to publish the
names, addresses or any other information about a student without
that student's permission to do so. Could the printing of the
passwd file be considered (even unauthorized) publication? Was
the school placed in a hazardous LEGAL position by the intrusion
into the system's security files?

It is said that the student printed the names out to satisfy his
own curiosity about how many of the students used the system as
compared to the student body. This data can be fairly easily 
found in many other ways. (cat /etc/passwd | wc -l will give a
quick count!). I'm not particularly paranoid, but it seems that
there must be a little more here than meets the eye. 

The data which is significant to the _system_ is the property of
the school. As such, there is question on whether or not it is
right to copy that data without their permission. Whether or not
that data may be a danger or harmless is neither here nor there.

Do we know what the agreement was as far as use of the computer
and/or what students are permitted to do? Did the student's 
action violate that agreement? Not was it harmful to do so, but
did it violate the agreement? The same may be asked about the
policies towards the schools information.

On the whole, I'm not going to jump on any bandwagon and write 
the school a letter --or become involved in any way. I don't 
know enough about what's going on here. I have been an admin for
quite a few years, and understand that policies, legalities, and
other considerations exist which many people don't know of or
have reason to understand. That one has a grasp of the software
or the hardware or the meeting of the two has no bearing on the
spectrum of system administration.

Because of this, I suggest that we all sit back and watch this
unfold --if there's anything to watch... After all, this is the
net.
