SULLIVAN SILENCED AT YALE
Secretary Disrupted by "Militant" Gays

 Using whistles, chants, hisses, and boos, a group of about 20
homosexual activists disrupted and drowned out a speech by
Secretary of Health and Human Services Louis Sullivan to over 250
people at Yale University.  According to reports in the Yale Daily
News, the protest was one of the _most disruptive demonstrations
against a campus speaker since the Vietnam era._
 Although Sullivan was able to deliver  his speech without
physical disruption from the protesters, the noise made by the
protesters made it almost impossible for him to be heard by
members of the audience.  The disruption is part of a string of
similar protests which reportedly began with the shouting down of
Sullivan during his speech to the International AIDS Convention in
San Francisco this past June.
 The protesters were comprised of members of the New Haven AIDS
Coalition To Unleash Power (ACT-UP) and the Gay, Lesbian, and
Bisexual Cooperative at Yale.  ACT-UP is a national organization
is notorious for its desecration of St. Patrick_s Cathedral in New
York City during a protest of John Cardinal O_Connor_s views on
homosexuality and abortion.  The Students for a Better America_s A
Student_s Guide to _Progressive_ Public Policy Organizations
describes ACT-UP as a _militant homosexual movement_ and notes
that the group is _stridently anti-Catholic and anti-Christian_
and _forms coalitions with...leftist causes ranging from
supporting Third World revolutionaries to opposing the Strategic
Defense Initiative._
 The subject of Sullivan_s speech at Yale, personal responsibility
in health care, did not specifically address the AIDS issue (it
was only mentioned briefly).  In his speech, Sullivan called his
visit _part of a larger dialogue that I am conducting with the
American people._  Yale student Patrick Greaney was quoted in the
Yale Daily News saying: _How dare he speak about _personal
responsibility_ when he_s responsible for thousands dying,
thousands getting infected?_
 Chants from the protesters during Sullivan_s speech included
_You_re saying _Don_t f---._  I_m saying f--- you._ and _Louis
Sullivan, you can_t hide.  We charge you with genocide._
Protesters also carried signs that showed a bulls-eye target on
Sullivan_s forehead, and labeled him _guilty._
 Joining the student demonstrators in the audience was Larry
Kramer, one of the co-founders of ACT-UP.  During Sullivan_s
introduction, Kramer yelled: _How dare this university invite this
murderer to speak?_
 Just before leaving the auditorium, Sullivan declared: _We spend
more money for AIDS than any other single disease._  This
statement was greeted with applause from many in the audience, and
Sullivan received a standing ovation as he left.  It was reported
in the Yale Daily News that the majority of the crowd was angry
with the protesters for attempting to deny Sullivan his right to
speak.
 At a press conference after the speech, Sullivan told reporters:
_There is no major idea in AIDS research not being funded right
now.  We have made more rapid progress in understanding the
disease and have organized a more rapid drug clearance program for
AIDS than any other disease._  ACT-UP leader Kramer later
responded: _This idiot says we_re spending enough money on AIDS,
more money than any other disease.  Of course we should f---ing
spend more money on AIDS, it_s a plague._
 In an article in Priorities, a publication of the American
Council on Science and Health, it has been reported that: _Since
early in this decade, we have spent, at the federal level, almost
$5 billion in research, and AIDS now receives more government
money than any other individual illness, more than heart disease,
cancer and stroke combined, despite the fact that the number of
diagnosed cases in the whole decade is but a minute fraction of
the cases of cancer, heart disease, and other chronic illnesses
that occur each year in the U.S._  It is also noted that Congress
has passed legislation designating an _unprecedented_ $4 billion
for AIDS patient care over the next five years.
 Responding to the actions of the protesters, Yale President Benno
Schmidt, Jr., said, _aside from the gross rudeness of this
behavior, such disruption is an assault on the principle of free
expression that lies at the heart of Yale_s academic mission._  He
also mentioned in initial reports in the Yale Daily News that
protesters may be _subject to the disciplinary processes of the
University._
 Many at Yale still believe, however, that the protesters were
justified in their actions.  In a commentary, students Eva
Kolodner and Timothy Dwight, who were among those causing the
disturbance, wrote: _While _civility_ is a nice idea, so often
exhortations for _civility_ are a means to maintain the positions
of people in power._  They also stated: _Near the end of the
event, a medical student called one of us a child.  If, in his
mind, being a child means refusing to listen to vacuous platitudes
about _traditional values,_ and yelling and screaming because our
government refuses to properly address the AIDS crisis, fails to
provide sufficient prenatal care, and ignores the decaying
educational system, drug abuse, and the homeless, then we proudly
accept the status of children._
