In article <1993May23.172002.18271@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>> Using whistles, chants, hisses, and boos, a group of about 20
>>homosexual activists disrupted and drowned out a speech by
>>Secretary of Health and Human Services Louis Sullivan to over 250
>>people at Yale University.  According to reports in the Yale Daily
>>News, the protest was one of the _most disruptive demonstrations
>>against a campus speaker since the Vietnam era._

Uh... Doesn't Yale have a free speech policy of some sort? Harvard's policy is
carefully drafted to protect the rights of speakers (in fact, I think it focuses
on speakers rather than protestors a little too much, but that's a different
story) - students who attempted to drown out a speaker rather than expressing
their disagreement can be evicted from an event and disciplined.

I'll be the first to admit that there's a gray area between legitimate protest 
and interfering with a speaker's own right to free speech. But based on the AIA
article, it looks like the Yale administration completely dropped the ball. 
Since instituting its policy, no Harvard student has been disciplined for
interfering with a speaker (and hopefully it will remain so after General Powell
speaks at Commencement). If people know the ground rules from the start, they're
much less likely to transgress them.

Can anyone from Yale recount what the university's policy is (I certainly hope
that one exists...)?

